module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_cap_set_here_surfaces_as_degradation_there

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, FoundOnRung, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { Mitigatable, StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef }

data a_cap_set_here_surfaces_as_degradation_there: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  // A LIMIT IS SET IN ONE PLACE AND ITS VIOLATION SURFACES IN ANOTHER, AS DEGRADATION RATHER THAN
  // REFUSAL. The place that set it has no signal; the place that pays has no idea a limit exists.

  identity: "a_cap_set_here_surfaces_as_degradation_there" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder,
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "TWO mechanisms, both unbuilt, and the second is not mine to grant: (1) the claim that exercises the terms refusal -- dag/test/claim/serving/serving_layout_witness.dag, target //dag/test/claim/serving:serving_layout_witness -- is ADMITTED TO THE REQUIRED FLOOR, a row of v2.workflow.required_floor.required_gate_authored_modules, which grows one exact module name at a time only on an operator ruling (gunbc.design_ruling::required_gate_rows_grow_by_operator_ruling); until then its green is authored evidence, executing on no lane that blocks anything, and the refusal can rot unheard. (2) The cap that binds a role is read back from the host as the cap that BINDS (the service's own MemoryHigh, not the slice above it) and joined against the terms the role's own rows sum -- gunbc.serving.serving_layout::serving_cgroup_terms -- so that a leaf cap below them is REPORTED by the identity read and REFUSED at placement, and the drop-in that carries it is rendered from those terms rather than typed on a host and read back as a fact. Until both exist the model refuses to budget against a short cap it is TOLD about, while the host can still be handed one by whoever edits the drop-in next"
    }
  },

  receipts: [
    "**INVALID STATE: a limit is declared in one place -- a cgroup drop-in, a queue depth, a service override -- and the cost of exceeding it appears somewhere else entirely: a different process, a different subsystem, a latency the operator attributes to the workload. Neither end is invalid, so nothing refuses; the only artifact is that the system runs badly, in a way whose cause is three layers away from the number responsible.**",

    "SPECIMEN ONE, the report of 2026-11-21 recounting srv4: the serving engine's own MemoryHigh was 40 GiB, below the ~22 GB expert working set plus everything else resident, and decode fell to 5.1 tok/s while each request read 41.5 GB from drive -- against 24.5-29.1 tok/s once the cap was raised to 56 GiB, where MemoryCurrent read 46.5 GB. A fivefold cliff, no alarm at the cap, no signal at the consumer. The repair landed the same day it was diagnosed and the figures survive as prose -- the comment on gunbc.serving.serving_layout::srv4_grant records 5.1 tok/s against 24.5/25.1/29.1 and the reads per request, and gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory::srv4_serving_engine_memory carries the new 56 GiB cap with the old shape in its provenance string. THE TREE RECORDED THE REPAIR AND NEVER THE RULE: nothing refuses a leaf cap set below the working set the role declares, on srv4 or anywhere else, and the same 40 GiB shape can be written again tomorrow by whoever edits the drop-in next.",

    "SPECIMEN TWO, same report: srv4 answered HTTP 429 at concurrency 4. Nothing in the model states that srv4 admits four concurrent requests and no more; the limit was discovered by colliding with it, in a lane that had come to do something else. A refusal at least announced itself -- which is why it was diagnosable at all -- and the row it belongs to is the one that names the shape, not a row about srv4.",

    "SPECIMEN THREE, srv4 again and the same cap, found 2026-11-24 by the operator's session: the 56 GiB the 2026-11-09 drop-in had set was never wrong for the term it was written to bound -- the routed-expert RAM tier, mmap-ed from NVMe under a cap (gunbc.design_ruling::srv3_experts_mmap_under_service_cap, operator direction 2026-11-09) -- it was wrong because FOUR terms share that one cgroup and nobody had added them up: the expert page cache, the conversation cache, the engine's admission floor, and the engine's own base and active slots. The engine's admission gate asks for free room inside the cgroup before it will park a conversation, and the page cache had taken all of it, so on every turn the engine logged 'conversation cache: skip parking (physical RAM admission; need N MiB plus 2560 MiB floor, M MiB available, source cgroup; evicted K, 0 still parked)' and the next turn re-read its whole prompt, about 90 seconds each. srv4 held about 50% warm (50.2% prompt-cache hits) where srv3, whose cap was 96 GiB, held 96%. The operator raised the cap live to 96G/112G on 2026-11-24 (drop-in 60-expert-l1-cap.conf, sha256 eb2b16e57eea76f2dd534c0bbb7aa016024462ad580cfb3836a1e8fad8bf581b, read 22:03-22:05 UTC); afterwards turns logged roughly '97815 reused + 1584 read', srv4 read 99.0% warm, about 33 tok/s per conversation, and no skip-parking line appeared in 21:40-21:55 UTC. STANDING: every figure in this specimen is an ad-hoc read taken by the operator's session on 2026-11-24 (systemctl show on the unit and its slice, the engine's own log and /metrics), carried as an operator-observed receipt and not as a deduced fact; no instrument in the tree re-reads them. THE THIRD SPECIMEN'S DIFFERENCE FROM THE FIRST TWO: the cap was not below the term it bounded, and the violation surfaced as a SILENT per-turn cost paid by the neighbour that was starved. The model had no better view than the operator did: gunbc.serving.serving_layout::serving_artifact_host_ram_charge billed an evictable artifact hold at its FLOOR, so the layout read a tier that occupies the 46.8 GiB it maps as reclaimable down to 22 GiB and handed the difference to the conversation cache as a retention budget the engine refused to spend -- the model and the kernel were making the same mistake, one layer apart, that reclaim is a loan a neighbour can pay itself back with.",

    "THE REPAIR THIS SPECIMEN PAID FOR, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT TOUCH: the 2026-11-09 ruling stands -- the expert tier is bounded, and bounding it is now what the model can actually say, because the tier is bounded by the OCCUPANCY the cap must leave it plus the FLOOR it may not give back, not by a number an operator guessed. An evictable hold states both (gunbc.serving.serving_layout::ServingArtifactHold ArtifactHeldEvictable { bound_by, floor, occupies }) and the budget bills the occupancy; what the cap must be is the SUM of the terms that share it (gunbc.serving.serving_layout::ServingCapTerms: expert occupancy, the replica's fixed RAM including the admission floor, the retention the requirement insists on, the rounding headroom), and a grant below that sum is not 'tight', it is unrenderable -- serving_allocate returns ServingLayoutRamShort naming each term. The 56 GiB cap re-rendered against srv4's own rows therefore REFUSES today; under the raised 96 GiB cap the same derivation configures, and the conversation cache is left 12 more conversations of room than the floor-as-charge reading used to leave it.",

    "WHAT THIS ROW IS NOT. It is not a second copy of gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::benchmark_warm_capacity_violation_read_as_engine_performance, whose defect is a benchmark receipt's STANDING (an admission violation read as throughput); and it is not a second copy of gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::a_capacity_default_sized_for_one_workload_shape_inherited_by_another, whose defect is the DIMENSION a capacity is stated in (a count of slots for a workload of several). This row is about WHERE the violation surfaces and what it surfaces AS: degradation, at a distance from the number, with the model's stated cap not even being the cap that binds. srv4's own row records that the slice limit it used to report no longer describes what runs -- the service's drop-in binds, and only a hand-read found which.",

    "THE MIS-STATED PREMISE THE REPORT CARRIES, RECORDED SO THE CLASS IS NOT BUILT ON IT: the report's third item said butch's 8 GiB /swap.img conflicts with a no-swap rule. It does not. gunbc.host.memory_envelope::memory_envelope_verdict judges swap at the SLOT's cgroup (memory.swap.current, .peak, .max) and the user-work slice sets MemorySwapMax=0 per run; gunbc.host.host_swap_backing consults HOST swap only as backing for a cap declared nonzero, and the runner slot's declared memory_swap_max is zero -- so /swap.img at priority -2 refuses nothing and blocks nothing -- and the tree already READS it without refusing: butch_a_strata_engine_memory records 'the host 8G /swap.img reads active, 0 used' beside a MemorySwapMax=0 it does treat as a cap. The axis (a presupposed limit resting on unobserved backing) is real and host_swap_backing owns it; this specimen does not belong to it.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: for every limit the fleet declares, name where its violation surfaces. If the answer is 'somewhere else, and slower' -- another subsystem, another process, a latency, an empty or short response -- the limit has no refusal and will be discovered by collision. The read side of the rule: the cap that BINDS is whatever the host's cgroup reports at the leaf that runs the role, which is frequently not the cap the model believes set.",

    "CEILING AND ITS TELL: StructurallyGuaranteed. A cap rendered FROM the terms and joined at admission against the leaf cgroup's own reading means an under-set cap cannot be carried silently, by the model or by the host: the role is refused until the cap and its terms agree. THE RUNG THIS ROW SITS ON IS Mitigatable, AND THE HONEST REASON IS THAT THE HALF WHICH IS BUILT IS NOT A GATE. Within the model a layout budgeted against a cap too small for its terms cannot be rendered at all -- it comes back Refused with the terms named (gunbc.serving.serving_layout::serving_cgroup_terms, serving_allocate) -- and everything downstream of a refused layout (grants, parked-tier verdicts, router projection) fails closed on it. What keeps that from being a rung is execution and enrolment: the only thing that exercises the refusal is dag/test/claim/serving/serving_layout_witness.dag, which runs under a locally built gunbc and under changed-witness selection on a PR that edits it and on NO required lane (v2.workflow.required_floor names no row for it, DESIGN's contract says 'a witness no lane executes is not a check', and that roster grows one module at a time only on an operator ruling). The cap the refusal measures against is a typed row (gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory::srv4_serving_engine_memory), never read back from the unit. So the invalid state stays writable on both sides of the boundary: the same 56 GiB can be typed onto srv5 tomorrow by whoever writes the next drop-in, and a regression in the terms sum would pass CI unheard. Claiming mechanically-preventable here would be rung inflation, which the ladder judges worse than sitting low; the tell that the ceiling is not even reached is specimen three itself -- the tree did not know the cap's terms until the operator raised the cap by hand and a session read the logs afterwards.",

    "NEXT TRIGGER: the identity read of gunbc.plans.desired_state_follows_the_machine (or its systemd read side) reporting a leaf cgroup cap below gunbc.serving.serving_layout::serving_cgroup_terms for the role that runs in it -- or the next term that shares an engine's cgroup entering the sum without appearing in ServingCapTerms (a second page cache, a cache the engine itself mmaps) -- or, on any host in either roster, the next cap raised to fix a slowdown the layout did not already refuse.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.plans.desired_state_follows_the_machine", decl_name: "desired_state_follows_the_machine_plan", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_host_memory", decl_name: "srv4_serving_engine_memory", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_layout", decl_name: "srv4_grant", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_layout", decl_name: "ServingArtifactHold", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_layout", decl_name: "serving_artifact_host_ram_charge", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_layout", decl_name: "ServingCapTerms", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_layout", decl_name: "serving_cgroup_terms", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.serving.serving_layout", decl_name: "serving_allocate", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.host.memory_envelope", decl_name: "memory_envelope_verdict", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.host.host_axis_caps", decl_name: "derived_runner_slot_cap_settings", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ]
}
